<p><strong>A spa or salon reduces paperwork by moving five things out of notebooks and spreadsheets and into one system: bookings, payments, discount approvals, staff attendance with payroll, and the daily close. Each is recorded once, when it happens, and every branch reads the same record.</strong></p>
<p>Most spas and salons in Nepal start with a paper register and a phone. That works for one room. With several therapists or a second branch, the same paper becomes lost slips, mismatched cash and a month-end that takes a weekend.</p>
<h2>1. Bookings: one diary instead of a register and a phone</h2>
<p>Phone, walk-in and online bookings all go into one schedule that books the staff member and the room together. Nobody rewrites the day from a notebook. See <a href="/features/booking/">conflict-free booking</a>.</p>
<h2>2. Payments: recorded at the counter, not rebuilt at night</h2>
<p>Cash, card and online payments, including part-payments, are recorded against the booking at the point of service. The evening cash count is then a check, not a reconstruction. See <a href="/features/payment-tracking/">payment tracking</a>.</p>
<h2>3. Discounts: approvals, not slips</h2>
<p>A staff member requests a discount with the exact amount, and it applies only after a manager approves it. Every step is kept, so there is no discount notebook to audit. See <a href="/features/discount-management/">discount management</a> and <a href="/blog/discount-leakage/">discount leakage</a>.</p>
<h2>4. Attendance and payroll: no month-end spreadsheet</h2>
<p>Attendance feeds <a href="/features/payroll/">payroll</a>: salary, absence deductions and therapist commission are calculated per branch from the same records. You review a draft run and finalise it to lock it. Read <a href="/blog/therapist-commission-payroll-nepal/">how to calculate therapist commission and payroll</a>.</p>
<h2>5. The daily close: a report, not an argument</h2>
<p>At the end of the day the revenue by payment method is already there. Close the day and it locks. See <a href="/blog/daily-reconciliation/">daily reconciliation</a> and the free <a href="/daily-close-checklist/">daily close checklist</a>.</p>
<h2>Across branches</h2>
<p>With more than one branch, paperwork multiplies. A shared system gives the owner every branch in one dashboard instead of a phone call to each manager. Read <a href="/blog/manage-multiple-spa-salon-branches-nepal/">how to manage multiple branches</a>, then <a href="#try-free" data-trial-open>try the free Zennly demo</a>.</p>
